From 5648a1439181d9163a0ddd92622391ff63c4b57f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zolotvok <zolotvok@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 08:06:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64ec: recompile blocks that keep trapping on unaligned
 atomics

On hosts without PR_ARM64_SET_UNALIGN_ATOMIC (stock 6.12 kernels), a guest
lock-prefixed op whose access crosses a 16-byte boundary makes the JIT's LSE
atomic (CASAL, CASPAL, LD<op>AL, SWPAL) raise an alignment fault.
HandleUnalignedAccess emulates these on every trap and never backpatches
them, so a hot loop pays a full exception round trip per access (under proot
on Android: a ptrace stop, about 50 us). Watch Dogs 2 took 5.44 M such traps
in one launch.

This extends the existing per-block recompile mechanism (as used for the mono
backpatcher block) instead of patching code in place:

- The ARM64EC exception handler counts handled JIT traps of the emulated
  atomic RMW class (IsEmulatedAtomicRMW) per guest block entry. At 16 traps it
  marks the entry (Context::MarkUnalignedAtomicBlock, under the unique code
  invalidation lock) and invalidates the page, like the mono detection does.
- CompileCode sets CPUBackend::UnalignedAtomicFallback for a marked entry.
  The JIT then emits each LSE atomic op as a flag-free 16-byte-crossing check
  (and/add/tbnz on TMP1) in front of two copies of the op: the unchanged fast
  copy, and a slow copy in which the atomic instruction is replaced by a call
  to the new dispatcher stub UnalignedAtomicStub, with the original
  instruction word left in place as data.
- The stub saves x0-x30, sp, NZCV and all vector registers (SVE256-aware via
  PushVectorRegisters), issues dmb ish, calls
  ContextImpl::UnalignedAtomicFallback, which runs
  HandleUnalignedAccess(IsJIT = false) on that exact instruction word, issues
  dmb ish, restores everything except the registers the instruction writes,
  and returns past the word.

Semantics: the slow path runs the same emulation code as the trap path
(HandleCASPAL/HandleCASAL/HandleAtomicMemOp with the same
StrictInProcessSplitLocks mutex), so atomicity is exactly what the stock trap
path gives: a single 128-bit CAS inside a 16-byte granule, the dual-CAS loop
across granules (serialised by the split-lock mutex when
StrictInProcessSplitLocks is set). Threads still running the old copy of a
block keep trapping into the same emulation, so both paths stay consistent.
Blocks that never reach the threshold are compiled exactly as before
(InstructionCountCI: 108/108 suites unchanged). Marked blocks bypass the disk
cache. FEX_UNALIGNED_ATOMIC_FALLBACK=0 disables the recompile,
FEX_UNALIGNED_ATOMIC_STATS=1 prints trap/recompile counts through
__wine_dbg_output even with FEX_SILENTLOG.

Also fixes the non-arm64 HandleUnalignedAccess stub signature so x86 CI
builds (InstructionCountCI) link.

Local Blobferry/DroidDeck patch, not for upstream submission (FEX does not
accept AI-generated code, see AGENTS.md).

